1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,343
Every large AI training run has a hidden tax.

2
00:00:03,343 --> 00:00:07,221
This video is about what it costs you — and how to stop paying it.

3
00:00:08,699 --> 00:00:13,416
Every twenty to thirty minutes, a training job stops to save a checkpoint

4
00:00:13,416 --> 00:00:16,129
— the model's weights and optimizer state.

5
00:00:16,129 --> 00:00:19,037
While that file writes, your GPUs do nothing.

6
00:00:19,037 --> 00:00:24,658
At H100 rates, a single large checkpoint can burn around five dollars of idle GPU time.

7
00:00:24,658 --> 00:00:28,340
Multiply that by every checkpoint, every node, every day.

8
00:00:29,699 --> 00:00:30,980
Here's why it happens.

9
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:34,066
Checkpoints are big — ten to a hundred-plus gibibytes

10
00:00:34,066 --> 00:00:38,723
— and they're written to durable shared storage, like NFS or Azure NetApp Files.

11
00:00:38,723 --> 00:00:42,566
Durable storage is the right place for them — it survives crashes.

12
00:00:42,566 --> 00:00:46,699
But it's slow, and the write sits on the training loop's critical path.

13
00:00:46,699 --> 00:00:49,494
The bigger the checkpoint, the longer the stall.

14
00:00:49,494 --> 00:00:51,589
At a hundred twenty-eight gigabytes,

15
00:00:51,589 --> 00:00:56,422
the durable path can hold your GPUs for two and a half minutes — every single time.

16
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:00,165
AIFS — the AI File System Accelerator

17
00:01:00,165 --> 00:01:04,145
— fixes this with one idea: decouple performance from durability.

18
00:01:04,145 --> 00:01:05,798
AIFS is a filesystem layer.

19
00:01:05,798 --> 00:01:09,778
Your training job writes to a normal directory — no code changes.

20
00:01:09,778 --> 00:01:15,228
Behind the mount, writes land on fast local NVMe first, and training resumes immediately.

21
00:01:15,228 --> 00:01:19,330
In the background, AIFS replicates the data to your durable storage

22
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:21,412
— which stays the source of truth.

23
00:01:21,412 --> 00:01:24,657
When every file in a checkpoint is safely replicated,

24
00:01:24,657 --> 00:01:29,127
AIFS writes a commit marker: this checkpoint is complete and trustworthy.

25
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,293
Let's watch it actually work.

26
00:01:32,293 --> 00:01:36,622
This is the real prototype — open source, written in C — running live.

27
00:01:36,622 --> 00:01:38,724
On the left, the local NVMe spool.

28
00:01:38,724 --> 00:01:40,827
On the right, the durable backend.

29
00:01:40,827 --> 00:01:45,527
We save a multi-file training checkpoint: model, optimizer, scheduler state.

30
00:01:45,527 --> 00:01:48,557
Watch the left side — the files appear instantly.

31
00:01:48,557 --> 00:01:50,907
The training job is already unblocked.

32
00:01:50,907 --> 00:01:54,309
Now watch the right — the same files arrive one by one,

33
00:01:54,309 --> 00:01:57,153
as the background replicator copies them over.

34
00:01:57,153 --> 00:02:02,719
And last of all, the commit marker — dot aifs committed — appears, sealing the checkpoint.

35
00:02:02,719 --> 00:02:06,739
If the node crashed mid-copy, that marker would never be written,

36
00:02:06,739 --> 00:02:11,191
and training would simply resume from the previous committed checkpoint.

37
00:02:11,191 --> 00:02:12,985
Durability, without the wait.

38
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:16,755
On real Azure hardware with local NVMe,

39
00:02:16,755 --> 00:02:21,554
the prototype sustains eight hundred seventy-nine megabytes per second on the fully

40
00:02:21,554 --> 00:02:22,305
durable path.

41
00:02:22,305 --> 00:02:26,006
A four-gibibyte checkpoint is locally safe in under five seconds

42
00:02:26,006 --> 00:02:30,516
— with replication finishing off the critical path, about half a minute later.

43
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:34,383
That speed converts directly into money.

44
00:02:34,383 --> 00:02:39,164
Seconds saved per checkpoint, times checkpoints per day, times GPUs per node,

45
00:02:39,164 --> 00:02:41,151
times what you pay per GPU hour.

46
00:02:41,151 --> 00:02:43,386
At a hundred twenty-eight gigabytes,

47
00:02:43,386 --> 00:02:46,988
that's roughly five dollars of avoided idle per checkpoint

48
00:02:46,988 --> 00:02:49,533
— before you multiply across the cluster.

49
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:56,155
If you run training jobs that checkpoint frequently, operate GPU infrastructure,

50
00:02:56,155 --> 00:03:00,622
or sell GPU capacity — AIFS turns storage speed into GPU efficiency.

51
00:03:00,622 --> 00:03:05,877
Try the pricing calculator to estimate your own savings, and find the prototype,

52
00:03:05,877 --> 00:03:08,504
the specs, and the benchmarks on GitHub.

53
00:03:08,504 --> 00:03:10,541
Stop paying the checkpoint tax.

